1063	read	\N	2024-01-18	0	8.0	A word of warning to everyone who wants to enter Kafka's Castle -  it will grind you and suck the strength out of you just like it did with our protagonist. But if you are one of those who like to test their nerves and their sanity, you may try climbing this mountain.<br/><br/>First of all, let me mention that it ends in the middle of the paragraph, abruptly, and so it doesn't have a clear ending. It is Kafka's last novel, so perhaps illness or death prevented him from finishing it.<br/><br/>The protagonist is K, a land surveyor who comes to work in a remote village on a mountain on top of which is a mysterious, inscrutable castle. It is a dark, otherworldly place where daylight lasts only for a few hours and winter reigns almost all the time. The problem arises immediately because the K is informed that they (castle officials) have not made any request to hire the surveyor. And so K tries in every way to reach the authorities who can help him.<br/><br/><i><blockquote class="quote-block">“Of course I'm ignorant, that remains true at all events and is extremely distressing for me, but it does have the advantage that the ignorant man dares more, so I shall gladly put up with ignorance and its undoubtedly dire consequences for a while, as long as my strength lasts.”<button class="quote-metadata-btn" contenteditable="false">📖</button></blockquote></i><br/>It can be interpreted in several ways, but it is inevitable that it is a picture of a rotten bureaucratic system that has spread in its inefficiency, unfairness, so every attempt by a person to find a solution to their problem, to find the way in that labyrinth of offices and officials, seems impossible and hopeless. The castle as a symbol of the inscrutability of higher authorities or maybe something else, who will know it.<br/><br/>The book has very little plot, all the focus is on the conversations that K has with the villagers He tries to portray himself as self-assured person convinced of a positive outcome, while everyone tries to tell him that he has no clue about the way things actually are. People in the village are scared and used to the system that the thought of going against it never crosses their mind. <br/><br/>Those back-and-forth conversations are mostly absurd, petty and pointless, it is excruciatingly difficult to find logic and meaning in them. Behavior of the characters is at times so crazy that it becomes comical. The whole novel is on the intersection of absurdity, comedy and tragedy. <br/><br/>K wants to fit in with the new environment, he desperately tries to make friends with the locals. But deep down he feels like he doesn't belong, not with elite nor with the villagers. All his efforts to be recognized as a deserving member of the community seem hopeless. He is surrounded with odd, unhelpful, distracting characters. Few do try to get close to him, but are they just using him for their own agenda? You cannot but draw a parallel with Kafka's personal life here. But K is not innocent and perfect either - he acts poorly towards those he thinks are beneath him so he can send a message of holding power, he neglects his love interests and is so stubborn in his thinking that he fails to really listen to others. <br/><br/><i><blockquote class="quote-block">“You misinterpret everything, even the silence.”<button class="quote-metadata-btn" contenteditable="false">📖</button></blockquote></i><br/>If I wasn't determined to read this, I would certainly have given up on the book more than once. It drove me crazy time and time again.<br/><br/>Now, as I try to shake up from the exhaustion the book inflicted, I realize here also lies the value of this classic. Because we as readers feel as hopeless, crushed and stupid as the protagonist feels in the jaws of a brutal and unfair system. In this sense, Kafka completely conveys the feeling to the reader. The topic so bleak couldn't have been portrayed in a more relatable way. It's not like we haven't felt that system on our skin. Symbols and metaphors are countless.<br/><br/>It was for the most part a feeling of extreme disbelief while reading, that for a moment turns into something enjoyable that quickly vanishes again. Would it have a happy ending if it was finished? I doubt it whole<br/><br/><i><blockquote class="quote-block">“I dream of a grave, deep and narrow, where we could clasp each other in our arms as with clamps, and I would hide my face in you and you would hide your face in me, and nobody would ever see us any more”<button class="quote-metadata-btn" contenteditable="false">📖</button></blockquote></i><br/>If you haven't read any Kafka, stay away from The Castle for a while. There is Metamorphosis and many other beautiful short stories to check out. And if you develop a tolerance and want to inflict some pain on yourself, then you can try this too. It won't be a pleasant experience but maybe, just maybe, it leaves you with a good feeling and a stronger character after some time is passed.<br/><br/><b>3.75/5 stars</b><br/>	f	3	0.8	t	2024-01-11 00:00:00+01	2026-01-13 11:20:56.497024+01	144	1	\N
