vom ranom romanu. Veoma je štur, precizan, neulepšan iako ima i divnih rečenica. Gotovo kao dnevnik, detaljan u prikazima toka dana i događaja. Kao izveštaj iz kratkog perioda od nekoliko nedelja koliko radnja zapravo traje. <br/><br/>Nema puno metafora niti impozantnih opisa, samo interakcije i afere među grupom poznanika koji su osetili želju da potpuno dožive život, makar ta želja bila posledica potrebe da se nešto zaboravi. <br/><br/><b>4/5</b>	f	2	0.6711409395973155	t	2024-08-17 00:00:00+02	2026-01-13 11:20:01.792602+01	522	1	\N
1012	read	\N	2024-02-05	0	8.0	This Russian novel is set in a desert somewhere between Tajikistan, Turkmenistan, and Uzbekistan. Nazar Chagataev, after completing his studies in Moscow, is sent back to his hometown by the Communist Party to try to save his people from extinction and build living conditions for them.<br/><br/>"Dzhan" is the name for his people, the desert wanderers who only know hunger, suffering, and death. Translated, "Dzhan" means soul (and slave), a name given to them mockingly by the wealthy, as they only have a soul to endure suffering and nothing more.<br/><br/><i><blockquote class="quote-block">"Abandon all hope, ye who enter."<button class="quote-metadata-btn" contenteditable="false">📖</button></blockquote></i> – this sign would be fitting for anyone heading in this direction. The semi-desert is a brutal place, with very little life surviving. Descriptions are striking, alternating between sand dunes and valleys, where people find water by digging and searching for sources, and chewing on sand for hydration. There is sparse grass, almost no other vegetation. People wander, searching for lost sheep, camels, tortoises, scavenger birds... The animals themselves resemble humans, emaciated, powerless, more like wilted plants.<br/><br/>The people, in their starvation, are completely numb. The line between life and death almost doesn't exist. There's no strength even for mourning the dead - in a sad way, their suffering is alleviated. It seems that even the poor animals would prefer someone to end their misery. The human spirit is dying within them, devoid of purpose, thought, or desire.<br/><br/>Nazar never loses hope for a moment; he only wants to make his people happy. With the help of a young girl, he tries to form a sustainable community and shake the human spirit.<br/><br/>Does belief continue to live in people despite the greatest hardships? A subdued, almost non-existent hope that flickers deep down, hope that things will get better one day.<br/><br/>Describing the atmosphere of the book at this point is likely unnecessary. It's extremely depressing and distressing, with a touch of optimism, it's a spiritual exploration. A somewhat absurd, modern biblical story that seems both real and abstract at the same time.<br/><br/><i><blockquote class="quote-block">“Everything in the existing world seemed strange to him; it was as if the world had been created for some brief, mocking game. But this game of make-believe had dragged on for a long time,for eternity, and nobody felt like laughing anymore.”<button class="quote-metadata-btn" contenteditable="false">📖</button></blockquote></i><br/>The book was banned by Stalin for over 50 years, and Platonov was blacklisted alongside Bulgakov and others. Warning: in some parts of the book, there's inhumane treatment of young girls.<br/><br/>The book is beautifully written, but with its highly poignant nature, it is not an easy read. <br/><br/><b>4/5 stars</b>	f	2	1.3071895424836601	t	2024-01-27 00:00:00+01	2026-01-13 11:20:01.830602+01	93	1	\N
